# Analyst Notes

Scheduled Tasks are one of the most common Windows persistence mechanisms used by both legitimate software and threat actors. Adversaries frequently create scheduled tasks to maintain persistence, execute malware after reboot, or repeatedly launch payloads while blending into normal Windows administration.

Although scheduled task creation is a legitimate administrative function, defenders should carefully review tasks executing from user-writable directories, launching PowerShell, Command Prompt, Rundll32, or other LOLBins. Correlating scheduled task creation with other endpoint telemetry significantly improves detection fidelity and helps identify post-exploitation activity.

